---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
---
<SiteLayout title="The Outer Solar System" description="Explore the Kuiper Belt, trans-Neptunian objects, scattered disc, detached objects, Centaurs, Oort Cloud and heliosphere." image="https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/oort-cloud-jpg.webp?w=1600">
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/solar-system/">Solar System Atlas</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>Outer Solar System</span></div>
  <header class="outer-hero">
    <div class="container outer-hero-inner">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">BEYOND NEPTUNE</span><h1>The regions<br /><em>beyond Neptune</em></h1><p class="lede">Beyond Neptune are several populations of icy bodies. Farther out, the solar wind meets interstellar space. The distant Oort Cloud is a proposed source of many long-period comets.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn" href="#kuiper">Start at the Kuiper Belt ↓</a><a class="btn secondary" href="#heliosphere">Explore the heliosphere ↗</a></div></div>
      <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia19/pia19952/PIA19952.jpg?w=1536&h=1536&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="NASA New Horizons view of Pluto, a large icy body in the Kuiper Belt." fetchpriority="high"/><figcaption>Pluto, seen by NASA's New Horizons spacecraft · Enhanced colour</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="container section" id="kuiper">
    <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">01 / 30–50 AU</span><h2>The Kuiper Belt</h2></div><p>A broad, flattened region beyond Neptune containing icy remnants from the Solar System's formation. Its main region begins near 30 AU and extends to roughly 50 AU.</p></div>
    <div class="region-feature">
      <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/solar/2023/09/o/Oort_Cloud.jpg?w=1000&h=1000&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint" alt="NASA artist's concept of the Solar System showing the Kuiper Belt as a flattened ring and the Oort Cloud as a distant spherical shell." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>NASA artist's concept · Shows both the Kuiper Belt and the hypothesised Oort Cloud; this is a schematic illustration, not an observation or a scale map. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/resource/oort-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Image source ↗</a></figcaption></figure>
      <div>
        <h3>What is it made of?</h3>
        <p>Kuiper Belt objects are cold mixtures of water ice, frozen volatile compounds, rock, dust and carbon-rich material. The exact mix differs from object to object and is inferred from spectra, density, colour, thermal measurements and spacecraft observations.</p>
        <p>Pluto, Haumea and Makemake are among the dwarf planets found in this distant region. Arrokoth, visited by New Horizons in 2019, is a contact-binary object that preserves clues to how small bodies assembled.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/kuiper-belt/facts/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA: Kuiper Belt facts ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="population-grid">
      <article><span>~30–50 AU</span><h3>Classical Kuiper Belt objects</h3><p>Objects in relatively stable orbits in the main belt. Many have low orbital inclinations and modest eccentricities.</p></article>
      <article><span>Resonant orbits</span><h3>Plutinos and other resonant bodies</h3><p>Objects whose orbital periods form repeating ratios with Neptune. Pluto is in a 3:2 resonance: two Pluto orbits for every three Neptune orbits.</p></article>
      <article id="scattered-disc"><span>Beyond the main belt</span><h3>Scattered disc</h3><p>Objects on more stretched and tilted orbits, strongly shaped by past and continuing gravitational encounters with Neptune. Some reach hundreds of AU and beyond.</p></article>
      <article><span>Detached orbits</span><h3>Detached objects</h3><p>Objects whose closest approach to the Sun is well outside Neptune's orbit. Sedna is a prominent example; its orbit reaches far beyond the main Kuiper Belt.</p></article>
      <article><span>Between giant planets</span><h3>Centaurs</h3><p>Small bodies with orbits between Jupiter and Neptune. Their paths evolve as they encounter the giant planets; some may become short-period comets.</p></article>
      <article><span>Shared planetary paths</span><h3>Trans-Neptunian binaries</h3><p>Many distant objects have companions. Binary systems help constrain mass, density and the way icy bodies formed and collided.</p></article>
    </div>
    <div class="callout"><strong>“Trans-Neptunian object” is a broad location-based term.</strong><p>It covers many different orbital families beyond Neptune. The Kuiper Belt is one part of that population; the scattered disc and detached objects are related groups, not exact synonyms for the whole region.</p></div>
  </section>

  <section class="section outer-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">02 / THE DISTANT COMET RESERVOIR</span><h2 id="oort-cloud">The Oort Cloud</h2></div><p>A scientific hypothesis supported by the orbits of long-period comets. It has never been directly imaged as a cloud.</p></div>
      <div class="oort-feature">
        <figure><img src="https://science.nasa.gov/wp-content/uploads/2016/05/oort-cloud-jpg.webp?w=1600" alt="NASA artist's concept of the Oort Cloud as a distant spherical shell surrounding the Solar System." loading="lazy"/><figcaption><strong>Artist's concept — not an actual photograph.</strong> No spacecraft or telescope has directly photographed the Oort Cloud itself.</figcaption></figure>
        <div class="oort-copy">
          <span class="status-label">HYPOTHETICAL STRUCTURE</span>
          <h3>What the Oort Cloud may contain</h3>
          <p>The Oort Cloud is proposed as a roughly spherical population surrounding the planetary system and Kuiper Belt. Its existence is inferred from the distribution of long-period comet orbits and models of how small icy bodies were scattered outward early in Solar System history.</p>
          <p>Its bodies are expected to contain mixtures of water ice, carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide, methane, ammonia, dust and rocky material, broadly similar to comet nuclei. The exact inventory, population and boundaries remain uncertain.</p>
          <div class="oort-distance">
            <div><b>~5,000 AU</b><span>Illustrative inner extent in NASA's model</span></div>
            <div><b>~100,000 AU</b><span>Illustrative outer extent in NASA's model</span></div>
            <div><b>~1.6 light-years</b><span>Outer distance in that model</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="small-note">These are broad model estimates, not measured edges. 100,000 AU is about 1.58 light-years. There is no known sharply defined outer surface.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA: Oort Cloud ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="oort-evidence">
        <figure><img src="https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/psd/photojournal/pia/pia26/pia26801/PIA26801.jpg?w=1024" alt="NASA Hubble observation of the distant long-period comet C/2014 UN271, Bernardinelli–Bernstein." loading="lazy"/><figcaption>Comet C/2014 UN271 · Hubble observation and derived nucleus analysis</figcaption></figure>
        <div><h3>How do we study the Oort Cloud?</h3><p>We study the comets that enter the inner Solar System. Their orbital paths, especially very long-period orbits, can reveal a distant source population. A comet image is a real observation of that comet — it is not a photograph of the cloud where it may have originated.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container" id="heliosphere">
    <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">03 / THE SOLAR WIND</span><h2>The heliosphere.</h2></div><p>The Sun continuously releases charged particles. This solar wind fills a vast region around the Sun and interacts with the thin gas and magnetic field between stars.</p></div>
    <div class="heliosphere-diagram card">
      <div class="heliosphere-visual" role="img" aria-label="Schematic diagram of the Sun, termination shock, heliosheath and heliopause. The boundary is asymmetric and the drawing is not to scale.">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 1000 410" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
          <defs>
            <radialGradient id="helio-sun"><stop offset="0%" stop-color="#fff4c5"/><stop offset="56%" stop-color="#efa84f"/><stop offset="100%" stop-color="#a85e36"/></radialGradient>
            <linearGradient id="helio-shell" x1="0%" y1="0%" x2="100%" y2="0%"><stop offset="0%" stop-color="#4b97bd" stop-opacity=".06"/><stop offset="65%" stop-color="#4b97bd" stop-opacity=".17"/><stop offset="100%" stop-color="#c7a777" stop-opacity=".07"/></linearGradient>
          </defs>
          <ellipse cx="465" cy="205" rx="410" ry="170" fill="url(#helio-shell)" stroke="#8dbbd0" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="7 8"/>
          <ellipse cx="425" cy="205" rx="285" ry="111" fill="none" stroke="#5d9bb7" stroke-width="2.5"/>
          <ellipse cx="380" cy="205" rx="216" ry="78" fill="none" stroke="#d7b77d" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="3 7"/>
          <circle cx="165" cy="205" r="25" fill="url(#helio-sun)"/>
          <g fill="#e5edf1" font-size="17" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif">
            <text x="165" y="258" text-anchor="middle">Sun</text>
            <text x="368" y="94" text-anchor="middle">Termination shock</text>
            <text x="523" y="55" text-anchor="middle">Heliosheath</text>
            <text x="806" y="115" text-anchor="middle">Heliopause</text>
            <text x="864" y="205" text-anchor="middle">Interstellar</text>
            <text x="864" y="229" text-anchor="middle">space</text>
          </g>
          <g stroke="#d9c08f" fill="none" stroke-width="1.4"><path d="M368 100L380 127"/><path d="M523 62L545 95"/><path d="M806 122L788 146"/></g>
          <g stroke="#7bb5d0" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none" opacity=".7"><path d="M202 180C280 140 340 130 405 134"/><path d="M202 205C285 205 340 205 404 205"/><path d="M202 230C280 270 340 280 405 276"/></g>
          <g fill="#7bb5d0"><path d="m398 128 10 6-10 5Z"/><path d="m398 199 10 6-10 5Z"/><path d="m398 270 10 6-10 5Z"/></g>
        </svg>
      </div>
      <p class="diagram-caption">Conceptual cross-section · Not to scale · The real heliosphere is three-dimensional, changing and not perfectly spherical.</p>
      <div class="helio-terms">
        <div><b>Termination shock</b><p>The solar wind slows abruptly from supersonic to subsonic flow. Voyager 1 crossed at about 94 AU in 2004; Voyager 2 at about 84 AU in 2007.</p></div>
        <div><b>Heliosheath</b><p>The outer region of slowed, heated solar wind between the termination shock and heliopause.</p></div>
        <div><b>Heliopause</b><p>The boundary where outward solar-wind pressure balances the surrounding interstellar medium. Voyager 1 crossed in August 2012 at 121.6 AU; Voyager 2 crossed in November 2018 at about 119 AU.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="callout"><strong>The heliopause is not the edge of the Sun's gravity.</strong><p>It marks a changing boundary of the solar-wind bubble. Distant objects can remain gravitationally bound to the Sun far beyond it, including the hypothesized Oort Cloud.</p></div>
    <a class="text-link" href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/interstellar-mission/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA: Voyager and interstellar space ↗</a>
  </section>

  <section class="section outer-dark">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">04 / VISITORS FROM ELSEWHERE</span><h2>Interstellar objects.</h2></div><p>Most Solar System objects orbit the Sun. A small number of observed visitors follow hyperbolic paths that show they came from outside the Solar System.</p></div>
      <div class="interstellar-grid">
        <article><span>2017</span><h3>1I/ʻOumuamua</h3><p>The first confirmed interstellar object observed passing through the Solar System. It was discovered after perihelion and showed a distinctive non-gravitational acceleration.</p></article>
        <article><span>2019</span><h3>2I/Borisov</h3><p>The first confirmed interstellar comet observed with a clearly visible coma. Its activity and composition could be compared with Solar System comets.</p></article>
        <article><span>2025</span><h3>3I/ATLAS</h3><p>The third confirmed interstellar visitor in NASA's current overview; observed as an active comet. Its trajectory is unbound to the Sun.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note">“Interstellar” describes the object's incoming trajectory and origin outside the Sun's gravitationally bound population. The census can change as new visitors are discovered.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <div class="source-panel">
      <div><span class="eyebrow">PRIMARY REFERENCES</span><h3>Sources and observations</h3><p>The scale estimates and boundaries on this page are model- or spacecraft-dependent. These references provide the detailed context.</p></div>
      <ul class="source-list">
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/kuiper-belt/facts/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Kuiper Belt facts and populations ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/oort-cloud/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Oort Cloud and its hypothetical status ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/interstellar-mission/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Voyager's termination-shock and heliopause crossings ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/hubble/hubble-confirms-largest-comet-nucleus-ever-seen/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA Hubble — Bernardinelli–Bernstein nucleus estimate ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/1p-halley/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — 1P/Halley ↗</a></li><li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/comets/3i-atlas/3i-atlas-facts-and-faqs/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — 3I/ATLAS facts and observations ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/10199-chariklo/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">NASA — Chariklo and its rings ↗</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    .outer-hero{background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0a1928,#163249 65%,#0a1a28);color:#f4f2ec;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.12)}.outer-hero-inner{display:grid;grid-template-columns:.9fr 1.1fr;gap:38px;align-items:center;padding:50px 0}.outer-hero .eyebrow{color:#d5b986}.outer-hero h1{font:500 clamp(2.6rem,4.8vw,4.6rem)/1.04 Georgia,serif;margin:15px 0}.outer-hero h1 em{font-style:normal;color:#d8bd8f}.outer-hero .lede{color:#c7d3dd;max-width:36rem}.outer-hero figure{margin:0;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);border-radius:10px;overflow:hidden}.outer-hero figure img{width:100%;aspect-ratio:16/10;object-fit:cover;display:block}.outer-hero figcaption{padding:9px 12px;font-size:.68rem;color:#bfccd6}.outer-hero .btn.secondary{background:transparent;color:#eff3f6;border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.28)}
    .region-feature{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:28px;align-items:center}.region-feature figure{margin:0;background:#0a1928;border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden}.region-feature img{display:block;width:100%;aspect-ratio:5/4;object-fit:contain;background:#0a1928}.region-feature figcaption{padding:9px 12px;color:#c1cdd7;font-size:.68rem}.region-feature h3,.oort-copy h3{font:500 1.8rem Georgia,serif;color:#17344c}.region-feature p,.oort-copy p{color:#556d80;font-size:.88rem;line-height:1.8}
    .population-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px;margin-top:25px}.population-grid article{border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:20px;background:#f6f8f9}.population-grid span{font-size:.68rem;font-weight:700;color:#98713e}.population-grid h3{font:500 1.15rem Georgia,serif;color:#18354d}.population-grid p{font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.7;color:#5a7083}
    .outer-dark{background:linear-gradient(145deg,#0b1b2b,#142e42);color:#f0f2f3;border-block:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.08)}.outer-dark .section-head h2{color:#f4f1e9}.outer-dark .section-head p{color:#c3ced8}.outer-dark .eyebrow{color:#d7bc8e}
    .oort-feature{display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.04fr .96fr;gap:30px;align-items:center}.oort-feature figure{margin:0;background:#fff;border-radius:9px;overflow:hidden}.oort-feature figure img{display:block;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:contain}.oort-feature figcaption{padding:10px 13px;color:#4f6477;font-size:.72rem}.oort-copy{padding:10px 0}.oort-copy h3,.outer-dark .oort-copy h3{color:#f5f1e9}.outer-dark .oort-copy p{color:#c1ccd7}.status-label{display:inline-block;padding:5px 8px;border:1px solid rgba(232,195,133,.4);border-radius:4px;color:#edcc94;font-size:.63rem;letter-spacing:.1em;font-weight:700}.oort-distance{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:8px;margin:20px 0}.oort-distance>div{padding:12px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14);border-radius:7px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.05)}.oort-distance b,.oort-distance span{display:block}.oort-distance b{font-size:1.05rem;color:#f1d39e}.oort-distance span{font-size:.67rem;color:#c2cfda;margin-top:4px}.outer-dark .small-note{color:#c3cfd8}.oort-evidence{display:grid;grid-template-columns:.8fr 1.2fr;gap:22px;align-items:center;margin-top:24px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.14)}.oort-evidence figure{margin:0}.oort-evidence img{width:100%;border-radius:7px;display:block}.oort-evidence figcaption{font-size:.68rem;color:#c0ccd5;margin-top:7px}.oort-evidence h3{font:500 1.4rem Georgia,serif;color:#f4f2ec}.oort-evidence p{color:#c1ccd7;font-size:.86rem;line-height:1.8}
    .heliosphere-diagram{padding:20px}.heliosphere-visual{background:radial-gradient(ellipse at 25% 50%,#142c40,#071421 76%);border-radius:8px;overflow:hidden}.heliosphere-visual svg{width:100%;height:auto;display:block}.diagram-caption{font-size:.7rem;color:#607588;margin:10px 0 18px}.helio-terms{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px}.helio-terms>div{padding:18px;background:#f4f7f8;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:7px}.helio-terms b{color:#173b56}.helio-terms p{font-size:.78rem;line-height:1.7;color:#5a7082;margin-bottom:0}.outer-dark .callout{background:rgba(255,255,255,.07);border-color:rgba(255,255,255,.15);color:#f4f1eb}.outer-dark .callout p{color:#c4d0da}.outer-dark .text-link{color:#efd29b}
    .interstellar-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:12px}.interstellar-grid article{padding:21px;border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.15);border-radius:8px;background:rgba(255,255,255,.05)}.interstellar-grid span{color:#dabe8d;font-size:.75rem}.interstellar-grid h3{color:#f4f1eb;font:500 1.3rem Georgia,serif}.interstellar-grid p{color:#c3cfd8;font-size:.81rem;line-height:1.7}
    @media(max-width:850px){.outer-hero-inner,.region-feature,.oort-feature,.oort-evidence{grid-template-columns:1fr}.outer-hero-inner{padding:36px 0}.population-grid{grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr)}.oort-distance{grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr}}
    @media(max-width:600px){.population-grid,.helio-terms,.interstellar-grid{grid-template-columns:1fr}.oort-distance{grid-template-columns:1fr}.heliosphere-diagram{padding:11px}.outer-hero .hero-actions{flex-direction:column;align-items:stretch}}
  </style>
</SiteLayout>
